---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CalcMoneyMarginMarkup from '@/components/tools/finance/CalcMoneyMarginMarkup.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="markup-calculator">
  <CalcMoneyMarginMarkup client:load variant="markup" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Markup is what you add to the cost</h2>
    <pre><code>selling price = cost × (1 + markup)
markup %      = (price − cost) ÷ cost × 100</code></pre>
    <p>
      Markup is a pricing instruction: start from what you paid and add a percentage to get the price.
      Buy at 8, apply a 150% markup, and sell at 20. It is the natural way to price when you have a cost
      list and need a price list, so wholesalers, distributors and trades almost always quote markup.
    </p>

    <h2>Keystone and the multiples</h2>
    <p>
      Retail has traditional names for common markups, and multipliers are faster to use than
      percentages:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[440px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Name</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Multiplier</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Markup</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Margin</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="tabular-nums">
          <tr><td>Cost plus a third</td><td style="text-align:right">× 1.33</td><td style="text-align:right">33%</td><td style="text-align:right">25%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Half up</td><td style="text-align:right">× 1.50</td><td style="text-align:right">50%</td><td style="text-align:right">33.3%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Keystone</td><td style="text-align:right">× 2.00</td><td style="text-align:right">100%</td><td style="text-align:right">50%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Keystone plus</td><td style="text-align:right">× 2.50</td><td style="text-align:right">150%</td><td style="text-align:right">60%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Triple</td><td style="text-align:right">× 3.00</td><td style="text-align:right">200%</td><td style="text-align:right">66.7%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      "Keystone" (double the wholesale cost) is the old default in general retail. It gives a clean 50%
      margin, and you can double a number in your head on a shop floor.
    </p>

    <h2>The markup you need for the margin you want</h2>
    <p>
      Most people need this calculation, and most get it wrong. For a target margin, the markup is
      <code>margin ÷ (1 − margin)</code>:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[340px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Target margin</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Markup required</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="tabular-nums">
          <tr><td>10%</td><td style="text-align:right">11.1%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>20%</td><td style="text-align:right">25.0%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>25%</td><td style="text-align:right">33.3%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>30%</td><td style="text-align:right">42.9%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>40%</td><td style="text-align:right">66.7%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>50%</td><td style="text-align:right">100%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>60%</td><td style="text-align:right">150%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>75%</td><td style="text-align:right">300%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Up to about 20%, the two numbers are close, so confusing them does little harm. Above 40% they
      separate fast. A business that thinks a 60% markup gives a 60% margin actually has 37.5%.
    </p>

    <h2>Cost-plus pricing: when it works and when it fails</h2>
    <p>
      Markup pricing works well for high-volume, low-differentiation goods where costs are known and
      competition sets a narrow band. It is fast, easy to explain to a customer, and hard to get very
      wrong.
    </p>
    <p>
      It fails in three places. It ignores what the customer will pay, so you lose money on distinctive
      products. It puts inefficiency into the price: if your costs are high, your price is high, and the
      market does not care. On slow-moving stock, it charges too little for capital that sits on a
      shelf for a year.
    </p>

    <h2>Building a markup up from the parts</h2>
    <p>
      A markup must cover more than profit. To set it, work back from the costs it must cover. This is
      better than a copy of a sector average:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Overhead recovery: rent, wages, software, insurance, spread across expected volume.</li>
      <li>Shrinkage and returns: a few per cent in most retail, much more in fashion.</li>
      <li>Markdowns: if a fifth of stock finally sells at 30% off, the full-price items must cover it.</li>
      <li>Payment and platform fees: 2–3% for cards, up to 15% on some marketplaces.</li>
      <li>Profit: what is left, and the only part that is yours.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Marking up a whole cost list</h2>
    <p>
      Paste a column of costs into the bulk panel (names optional, comma or tab separated) and set the
      markup once. Each line shows its price, its profit and the margin that markup gives. Export the
      result as CSV, Excel or PDF for your price list.
    </p>

    <h2>Mark up the net figure</h2>
    <p>
      Apply markup to the cost without recoverable VAT or GST, and add sales tax to the finished price
      afterwards. A markup on a tax-inclusive cost adds the markup on the tax to the price. If you are
      VAT-registered, that tax was never a cost to you.
    </p>

    <h2>Nothing is uploaded</h2>
    <p>
      Your cost list stays on your machine. The markup calculator is static HTML and a small JavaScript
      island, and all calculations, tables and exports also work offline.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
